content.text:
B1 completed successfully. Deviations are reasonable (pnpm-workspace.yaml for pnpm 11 allowBuilds, @types/node, etc.). Interesting: AGENTS.md mentions `plugins/feature-extractor/` — but my revised plan uses `native/android/` + sync script instead of a local plugin package. Hmm — the AGENTS.md was written by B1 referencing my original spec which said `plugins/feature-extractor/`. I decided during planning to switch to registerPlugin + native source copy approach. I should keep it consistent. Let me reconsider: spec says `plugins/feature-extractor/` local plugin. Options:

1. Follow spec: local Capacitor plugin package at plugins/feature-extractor/ with its own package.json, android/ gradle module, TS web part. Consumed via file: dependency. This is the cleaner, evolvable structure and matches spec + AGENTS.md.
2. My simpler alternative: native/android/ + copy script.

Local plugin with file: dep: web part needs build (tsc → dist). With pnpm + Vite, TS source can be imported directly if package.json main points to src/index.ts? Vite handles TS in linked workspace deps if not excluded... file: deps are symlinked, Vite processes them (not in node_modules exclude since symlink resolves outside). Actually Vite dev handles it; vite build (rollup) also compiles TS via esbuild? Rollup needs plugins for TS — @vitejs/plugin-vue doesn't compile arbitrary TS in deps... Actually Vite uses esbuild to transform .ts files regardless of location (esbuild handles TS). Vite transforms all imported .ts through esbuild. So a file: plugin with main: "src/index.ts" would work. But Capacitor convention: web part dist. For robustness and convention, build the plugin web part with a tiny tsc step.

For the demo, simplest robust: plugin web part is just type definitions + registerPlugin wrapper — trivial TS, compile with tsc to dist (no rollup needed). Plugin android part: build.gradle with TFLite deps + Kotlin class. Version control includes plugin's android source (it's inside plugins/, not the generated android/ app project). cap sync links plugin android automatically via capacitor.settings.gradle. Gradle assembleDebug will compile it. 

This matches spec — go with plugins/feature-extractor/ local plugin. AGENTS.md constraint line "插件归 plugins/feature-extractor/" is consistent. Good, keep spec approach.

Now: freeze contracts (I write contracts.ts myself), then dispatch B2‖B3‖B4 in one message.

First verify B1's work myself (复跑)! DSH rule 3: 子代理声称跑过的命令，派发方必须独立复跑. But B1 is a scaffold batch — its outputs feed later batches; final integration re-run happens at the end. Rule says窄验证复跑 for claimed commands. I'll re-run install+build quickly now (fast: 414ms install, 617ms build per report). Cheap to verify now.

Then write contracts.ts. Then dispatch 3 batches.

contracts.ts content:

```ts
// 识别模块冻结契约（派发方定死，各批次不得修改本文件）
// 修改需经派发方同意并同步全部批次。

/** SKU 档案记录 */
export interface SkuRecord {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  createdAt: number;
}

/** 特征库条目：一条向量隶属一个 SKU */
export interface VectorEntry {
  skuId: string;
  vector: number[];
}

/** SKU 级命中结果：score 为该 SKU 全部向量的最高余弦相似度，0..1，降序 */
export interface MatchResult {
  skuId: string;
  score: number;
}

/** matcher.ts —— 纯函数，禁止依赖 Capacitor / DOM / localforage */
export function cosineSimilarity(a: number[], b: number[]): number;
export function topNBySku(query: number[], entries: VectorEntry[], n: number): MatchResult[];
```

Hmm, contracts.ts with function declarations can't have implementations... A .ts file with `export function cosineSimilarity(...): number;` without body is invalid unless declare. Better: contracts.ts contains only interfaces/types + documented signatures as types:

```ts
export type CosineSimilarity = (a: number[], b: number[]) => number;
export type TopNBySku = (query: number[], entries: VectorEntry[], n: number) => MatchResult[];

export interface ExtractResult { vector: number[]; elapsedMs: number; backend: 'gpu' | 'cpu'; }
export interface ModelInfo { backend: 'gpu' | 'cpu'; inputSize: number; vectorDim: number; }

export interface VectorStore {
  listSkus(): Promise<SkuRecord[]>;
  createSku(name: string): Promise<SkuRecord>;
  addVectors(skuId: string, vectors: number[][]): Promise<void>;
  getEntries(): Promise<VectorEntry[]>;
  deleteSku(skuId: string): Promise<void>;
  fillRandom(skuCount: number, vectorsPerSku: number, dim: number): Promise<void>;
  clearAll(): Promise<void>;
}
```

And plugin contract:
```ts
export interface FeatureExtractorPlugin {
  loadModel(): Promise<ModelInfo>;
  extract(options: { imagePath: string }): Promise<ExtractResult>;
}
export const FEATURE_EXTRACTOR_PLUGIN_NAME = 'FeatureExtractor';
```

Behavioral semantics need documenting in comments: topNBySku returns at most n entries sorted desc by score; ties → stable by skuId? cosine on zero vectors → return 0? Empty entries → []. These belong in matcher tests (B2 TDD). Put semantics in contract comments.

B2 (recognition module): files src/recognition/{matcher.ts, vectorStore.ts, extractor.ts} + tests/matcher.test.ts (+ maybe tests/vectorStore.test.ts with fake-indexeddb — adding dep fake-indexeddb devDep; allow B2 to add it to package.json devDependencies — but package.json is B1's file... B2 needs to edit package.json to add fake-indexeddb. Whitelist extension: allow B2 to edit mobile-app/package.json (devDeps only) and pnpm-lock (via pnpm add -D). No other batch edits package.json (B3 adds file: dep for plugin! Conflict!). 

Hmm: B3's local plugin requires adding `"@demo/feature-extractor": "file:plugins/feature-extractor"` to mobile-app/package.json dependencies. B2 also wants to add fake-indexeddb. Both edit package.json + lockfile → contract面/lockfile conflict. Rule: lockfile edits serialized. Options: (a) I make both edits myself before dispatch (add file: dep + fake-indexeddb, run pnpm install once); (b) serialize B2/B3. Option (a) is cleanest: contract面（package.json deps）由派发方定死. I'll do it myself: after B3? No—file: dep requires plugin package.json to exist for install... I can add dependency entry before plugin exists? pnpm install would fail (file: target missing). Sequence: B3 creates plugin package.json first, then install. 

Alternative: avoid lockfile churn — B2: skip fake-indexeddb; test vectorStore against an in-memory localforage stub? Simpler: vectorStore.test.ts uses localforage with memory driver? localforage in Node has no backend... vitest environment jsdom provides localStorage → localforage falls back to localStorage driver. Add jsdom devDep — still package.json edit.

Decision: I will pre-edit package.json myself adding `jsdom` (devDep) and the file: plugin dependency placeholder? file: dep needs plugin package to exist at install time.

Cleaner sequencing:
1. Me: write contracts.ts.
2. Dispatch B3 (plugin package incl. its package.json, TS web part, android module) — narrow verify: plugin tsc build only (no install into app yet). No package.json edit of mobile-app by B3.
3. B2 in parallel: matcher + tests (pure, no deps needed — no package.json edit!). vectorStore: implement with localforage; tests: only matcher.test.ts (contract says纯函数可测; vectorStore tested implicitly via integration later? TDD skill demands tests for new functions... vectorStore is mostly localforage glue; to keep TDD honest without package.json churn, B2 can write vectorStore with an injectable storage backend and test with in-memory Map backend! That satisfies TDD with zero new deps: `createVectorStore(backend?: StorageBackend)` defaulting to localforage. Good design anyway.
4. B4 views in parallel — no package.json edits.
5. After B3 done, me: add `"@demo/feature-extractor": "file:plugins/feature-extractor"` to mobile-app/package.json, run pnpm install (lockfile update by me). extractor.ts (B2) imports from contracts? extractor.ts needs the plugin: `registerPlugin<FeatureExtractorPlugin>(FEATURE_EXTRACTOR_PLUGIN_NAME)` from '@capacitor/core' directly — no dependency on plugin package for the web side! Then plugin package web part becomes unnecessary entirely. The plugin package only needs android/ native code + package.json for Capacitor CLI discovery. Capacitor CLI discovers plugins by scanning package.json deps for capacitor plugin packages; plugin's package.json needs "capacitor" field pointing to android src. The app must depend on it for sync to include it. So: B3's plugin = package.json + android/ only (+ optional src types re-exporting contracts). extractor.ts uses registerPlugin directly — no TS import of plugin needed. 

So B2 doesn't touch package.json at all. B3 doesn't touch mobile-app/package.json (I add file: dep at integration). B4 doesn't. Lockfile only touched by me. All three parallel-safe.

B4 views need extractor.ts/vectorStore.ts/matcher.ts APIs — from contracts.ts (frozen) — allowed read: contracts.ts + spec. B4 writes only the 3 view files. B4 narrow verify: `pnpm build` (vue-tsc) — but B4's build runs while B2's files may not exist yet... B4 imports from src/recognition/extractor etc. If B2 hasn't finished, typecheck fails! Parallel write with cross-imports: B4 depends on B2's files existing. Options: B4 imports types from contracts.ts (exists) and runtime modules from src/recognition/* — vue-tsc needs those files present. To keep parallel, B4 could import only contracts types and call runtime through a tiny facade that I freeze too? Runtime imports still need B2's files.

Serial fallback: B2 first, then B4. Time cost moderate. Or: I write stub implementations of extractor.ts/vectorStore.ts/matcher.ts along with contracts (throw new Error('not implemented')), B2 replaces matcher+vectorStore, B4 writes views against stubs, typecheck passes anytime. Stubs = my contract files. That keeps full parallelism: B2 whitelist = src/recognition/{matcher.ts,vectorStore.ts,extractor.ts} full replace + tests/. B4 whitelist = views only. 

matcher stub returning [] would make views typecheck. OK — I'll write contracts.ts + three stub files (explicit "STUB — B2 replaces"). Minimal code, contract-level. Acceptable for main control (contract freeze duty).

Native verification with gradle: B3 narrow verify = plugin tsc? Plugin has no TS web part now. B3 verify: `npx cap add android` + cap sync + gradlew assembleDebug? That requires app package.json to include file: dep — which I'm adding at integration... Circular. Resolve: B3 creates plugin files only; narrow verify = none on-device, just files. Then integration (me): add dep → install → cap add android → cap sync → gradlew assembleDebug. Gradle build might take 10+ min first time (downloads gradle-8.x distribution ~130MB). Run in background job.

Actually better: give B3 the gradle verification to shift load: allow B3 to edit mobile-app/package.json to add file: dep (whitelist addition, B3 only batch editing package.json) + run install + cap add android + cap sync + gradlew assembleDebug in background. B2/B4 don't touch package.json/lockfile — B3 solely owns them this round. Parallel-safe per rule 5 (lockfile single-writer). B4's vite build vs B3's gradle build: different dirs (dist vs android/), OK. B3's pnpm install while B4 runs vite build: pnpm install modifies node_modules mid-build — risk of transient failures. Acceptable? Could cause flaky failure attribution. Hmm. DSH: 子代理不得并发跑共享产物的全量构建 — node_modules is shared. To be safe: B3 instructed to run `pnpm install` BEFORE B4 starts? Can't sequence within same message easily... 

Simplest robust plan: 
- Message 1 (parallel): B2 (recognition+tests, no package.json) ‖ B3 (plugin files only + model fetch script + attempt model download; no gradle yet).
- After B3 returns: me — add file: dep, install, cap add android, cap sync, gradle build (background).
- B4 in parallel with B2/B3 using my stubs — vite build during this phase has no node_modules churn. ✓
- Integration re-run by me at end.

Model download: B3 attempts (Kaggle/TF Hub). Model needed for runtime only, not compile? Kotlin compiles without model; app fails loadModel at runtime without it. assets/*.tflite gitignored; fetch script downloads. B3 tries download; if blocked, documents.

Let me also double-check capacitor plugin local file: dep format and gradle include: capacitor sync generates capacitor.settings.gradle including ':capacitor-android' etc. and plugin modules via relative path from node_modules. file: deps are symlinked into node_modules — works.

TFLite deps in plugin build.gradle: org.tensorflow:tensorflow-lite:2.14.0 (or lite-gpu:2.14.0), tensorflow-lite-support for bitmap preprocessing (optional; can do manual). Keep: tensorflow-lite + tensorflow-lite-gpu. Versions: use 2.14.0 stable (widely mirrored). GPU delegate lib: org.tensorflow:tensorflow-lite-gpu:2.14.0.

minSdk: Capacitor 7 requires minSdk 23+, compileSdk 35? AGP version in generated android project: Cap 7 uses AGP 8.x, gradle 8.x, compileSdk 35 — needs SDK platform 35; we have 32/34/37.0(!). android-37.0? Odd naming (37.0?). Risk: compileSdk 35 missing → gradle build fails with license/missing platform. Can install via sdkmanager? sdkmanager exists under cmdline-tools? Check later at integration; if missing, `sdkmanager "platforms;android-35"` — needs cmdline-tools. We saw dirs: build-tools, platforms, platform-tools, emulator... no cmdline-tools listed! Listed: .temp, build-tools, emulator, environments, licenses, platform-tools, platforms, sources. No cmdline-tools → can't install platforms easily. android-37.0 might BE the newest (Android 15 is 35; 36 = Android 16; 37.0 weird). Cap 7.6 might use compileSdk 36? Capacitor 7 targets compileSdk 35 by default I think. Hmm. Mitigation: override compileSdk in android/variables.gradle or plugin build.gradle to 34 (present) if 35 missing. Instruct B3/integration: if compileSdk missing, set to highest installed. I'll handle at integration.

Also gradle needs to download distribution + AGP + deps from network — pnpm worked, so network OK (maybe via proxy/mirror). Gradle may need maven central access — likely fine.

OK. Now act:
1. Re-run B1 verification (install+build) — quick.
2. Write contracts.ts + 3 stubs.
3. Dispatch B2‖B3‖B4.

Step 1 and 2 can be same message? pwsh + write calls — write depends on nothing from pwsh. Yes, parallel. But contracts.ts under mobile-app/src/recognition/ — new dir, write creates it.

Stub contents:

contracts.ts — as above with semantics comments.

